        <p class="profile-detail-region">Great work · Central &amp; East Asia</p>
        <h1 class="profile-detail-name">Potala Palace</h1>
        <div class="info-stats" style="margin-top:10px;">
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Period</span><span class="info-stat-val">1645 CE – present</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Made by</span><span class="info-stat-val">Begun in 1645 at the order of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/5th-dalai-lama">5th Dalai Lama</a>, Ngawang Lozang Gyatso, advised by Konchog Chophel, the Thirty-fifth <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ganden-monastery">Ganden</a> Tripa of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gelug">Gelug</a> school, on the site of an earlier palace attributed to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/songtsen-gampo">Songtsen Gampo</a></span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Form</span><span class="info-stat-val">A dzong-style complex of 13 stories at about 3,700 metres, measuring 400 metres east to west and 350 metres north to south, with sloping stone walls averaging 3 metres thick, a White Palace and a Red Palace, more than 1,000 rooms, 10,000 shrines and some 200,000 statues</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Where it is now</span><span class="info-stat-val">On Marpo Ri, the Red Hill, in the centre of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lhasa">Lhasa</a> Valley, in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tibet">Tibet</a> Autonomous Region of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a></span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Known for</span><span class="info-stat-val">the winter palace and residence of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dalai-lama">Dalai Lamas</a>; the White Palace and the crimson Red Palace; chapels holding parts of the earlier palace; the Kalachakra and Yamantaka mandalas; thangkas hoisted on its south front at the Sertreng Festival; inscription on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco">UNESCO</a> World Heritage List in 1994</span></div>
        </div>
