    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/5th-dalai-lama">5th Dalai Lama</a> began the present palace in 1645 on the site of an earlier palace attributed to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/songtsen-gampo">Songtsen Gampo</a>, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dalai-lama">Dalai Lama</a> and his government moved into the White Palace in 1649.</li><li>The outer structure was built in 3 years, but the interior and its furnishings took 45, and construction lasted until 1694. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/5th-dalai-lama">5th Dalai Lama</a>’s death in 1682 was kept hidden so that the palace could be finished.</li><li>The palace was the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dalai-lama">Dalai Lamas</a>' residence until 1959 and became a museum after the annexation of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tibet">Tibet</a> by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peoples-republic-of-china">People's Republic of China</a>; it was inscribed on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco">UNESCO</a> World Heritage List in 1994.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/copper">Copper</a> was poured into the foundations of the Potala to protect it from earthquakes, and its sloping stone walls are 5 metres thick at the base.</p></aside>
    </section>

